1
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:24,760
Many laboratory procedures, especially biological and biochemical ones, require the precise

2
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:28,180
transfer of small quantities of liquids.

3
00:00:28,180 --> 00:00:33,460
Glass pipettes, which are commonly used in chemistry labs, are simply not accurate when

4
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:36,500
working with volumes that are less than one milliliter.

5
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:41,860
The best method of transferring small volumes of liquids involves the use of an automatic

6
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:44,360
pipette.

7
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:49,760
An automatic pipette consists of a piston that displaces air to create a vacuum when

8
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,760
its plunger is pressed.

9
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:56,720
The tip of the automatic pipette is then placed into the sample vial and the plunger is slowly

10
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:58,200
released.

11
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,920
This action draws liquid into the tip.

12
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:06,280
Finally, this liquid is expelled into the desired container by slowly depressing the

13
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:10,100
plunger a second time.

14
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:13,700
As shown, a variety of automatic pipettes exist.

15
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:19,040
This video will demonstrate automatic pipetting technique using the pipette men.

16
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:24,740
Using good technique will ensure accuracy, which is defined as the closeness of the dispensed

17
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:30,100
volume to the set volume, and precision, which is defined as the reproducibility of

18
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:34,510
individual measurements between your samples.

19
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:41,550
A pipette men consists of a plunger button, a tip ejector button, a volume adjustment knob,

20
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:45,990
a volume indicator, and a plastic shaft.

21
00:01:45,990 --> 00:01:49,470
Pipette men are available in six different sizes.

22
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:51,910
Five of them are depicted here.

23
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:57,110
The number on the plunger button indicates the maximum volume in microliters that each

24
00:01:57,110 --> 00:02:00,190
pipette men is capable of transferring.

25
00:02:00,190 --> 00:02:06,590
For example, the P20 has a possible volume range of 0 to 20 microliters.

26
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:11,830
However, transferring volumes at the low end of the range is not recommended because the

27
00:02:11,830 --> 00:02:15,910
standard deviation is too high to ensure precision.

28
00:02:15,910 --> 00:02:22,310
Thus, the minimum amount of liquid that can be transferred in microliters is 0.1 for the

29
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:34,550
P2, 0.5 for the P10, 2 for the P20, 10 for the P100, 50 for the P200, and 100

30
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:37,750
for the P1000.

31
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:42,790
Now that we've covered what an automatic pipette is and how it works, we'll take a detailed

32
00:02:42,790 --> 00:02:46,990
look into how the pipette men is used in the laboratory.

33
00:02:46,990 --> 00:02:54,550
Let's say, for example, your procedure requires you to transfer 153.5 microliters of sample.

34
00:02:54,550 --> 00:03:00,910
The first step involves the selection of the pipette men with an appropriate volume range.

35
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:04,270
In this example, a couple of choices exist.

36
00:03:04,270 --> 00:03:11,710
The P200 has a recommended volume range of 50 to 200 microliters, while the P1000 has

37
00:03:11,710 --> 00:03:16,270
a recommended range of 100 to 1000 microliters.

38
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:22,310
Remember, the lower end of a volume range always has a higher standard deviation.

39
00:03:22,310 --> 00:03:26,590
Therefore, the P200 is the better choice.

40
00:03:26,590 --> 00:03:31,510
The second step is the selection of the proper disposable plastic tip.

41
00:03:31,510 --> 00:03:33,430
Three tip sizes exist.

42
00:03:33,430 --> 00:03:42,110
The large blue tips, which fit the P1000, the medium yellow tips, which fit the P200, P100,

43
00:03:42,110 --> 00:03:48,910
and P20, and the small white tips, which fit the P10 and P2.

44
00:03:48,910 --> 00:03:53,710
Because we're using the P200, we're going to need the yellow tips.

45
00:03:53,710 --> 00:04:00,470
Using the proper tip is essential for preventing leaks and promoting accurate delivery of liquids.

46
00:04:00,470 --> 00:04:06,150
After selecting the correct pipette men end tip, set the desired transfer volume.

47
00:04:06,150 --> 00:04:13,710
For the smaller pipette men, P2 to P200, the volume set on the indicator translates exactly

48
00:04:13,710 --> 00:04:16,670
into the volume that will be delivered.

49
00:04:16,670 --> 00:04:24,270
This P200 is set to deliver a volume of 125.9 microliters.

50
00:04:24,270 --> 00:04:30,350
For the P1000, the volume set on the indicator must be multiplied by 10 to determine the

51
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:33,270
actual volume that will be transferred.

52
00:04:33,270 --> 00:04:40,870
This P1000 is set to deliver a volume of 1,259 microliters.

53
00:04:40,870 --> 00:04:46,210
To correctly adjust the volume setting, turn the volume adjustment knob to one third of

54
00:04:46,210 --> 00:04:49,230
a revolution past the desired volume.

55
00:04:49,230 --> 00:04:54,390
Then slowly move the dial back down to the desired setting.

56
00:04:54,390 --> 00:05:00,070
Always approach the desired volume by dialing downwards to prevent mechanical backlash and

57
00:05:00,070 --> 00:05:03,310
to ensure precise volume transfer.

58
00:05:03,310 --> 00:05:08,750
When adjusting the volume of a pipette men, take care not to rotate the dial past the

59
00:05:08,750 --> 00:05:10,190
maximum.

60
00:05:10,190 --> 00:05:14,910
You cannot transfer volumes above the range of the pipette men.

61
00:05:14,910 --> 00:05:17,730
Now it's time to transfer your sample.

62
00:05:17,730 --> 00:05:23,670
You will need your appropriately sized pipette men set to the desired volume, the correctly

63
00:05:23,670 --> 00:05:33,230
sized tips, a rack, the new container, a Kim wipe, and a waste container.

64
00:05:33,230 --> 00:05:38,190
Attach a disposable tip to the pipette men by pressing its plastic shaft firmly into

65
00:05:38,190 --> 00:05:40,430
the tip with a twisting motion.

66
00:05:40,430 --> 00:05:44,870
This action should ensure an airtight seal and prevent leaks from occurring.

67
00:05:44,870 --> 00:05:49,910
Always use a new sterile tip for each sample and never let the tip touch anything except

68
00:05:49,910 --> 00:05:51,670
sample.

69
00:05:51,670 --> 00:05:57,630
To draw sample into the plastic tip, depress the plunger until resistance is felt.

70
00:05:57,630 --> 00:06:01,790
Hold the pipette men vertically and immerse it into the sample.

71
00:06:01,790 --> 00:06:07,590
After the tip is immersed, slowly release the plunger to draw liquid into the tip.

72
00:06:07,590 --> 00:06:12,890
Wait a few seconds to ensure that the full volume of sample is drawn into the tip.

73
00:06:12,890 --> 00:06:19,450
As a guide, wait one second for the P2 to P200 pipette men and two to three seconds

74
00:06:19,450 --> 00:06:21,470
for the P1000.

75
00:06:21,470 --> 00:06:27,270
The larger the volume, or the more viscous the sample, the longer you should wait.

76
00:06:27,270 --> 00:06:31,990
Remove the pipette men from the sample and carefully wipe excess liquids from the sides

77
00:06:31,990 --> 00:06:33,490
of the tip.

78
00:06:33,490 --> 00:06:38,110
To dispense the liquid in the pipette men, touch its tip to the side wall of a new

79
00:06:38,110 --> 00:06:43,310
container and slowly depress the plunger to the first stop.

80
00:06:43,310 --> 00:06:49,290
Wait a few seconds to ensure complete sample flow, then press the plunger to the second

81
00:06:49,290 --> 00:06:52,670
stop to expel any remaining liquid.

82
00:06:52,670 --> 00:06:57,550
With the plunger fully depressed, carefully remove the tip from the container by sliding

83
00:06:57,550 --> 00:06:59,830
it up the container wall.

84
00:06:59,830 --> 00:07:06,030
The importance of sliding the tip along the wall increases as the volumes become smaller.

85
00:07:06,030 --> 00:07:10,870
Finally, allow the plunger to return to the up position.

86
00:07:10,870 --> 00:07:15,990
If you are done working with a particular sample, press the tip ejector button to release

87
00:07:15,990 --> 00:07:20,530
the tip into the proper waste receptacle.

88
00:07:20,530 --> 00:07:26,050
Several other guidelines will help to ensure accuracy and precision when transferring liquids.

89
00:07:26,050 --> 00:07:31,450
First, use a fresh tip for each sample to avoid contamination.

90
00:07:31,450 --> 00:07:35,010
Second, pre-rinse each new tip.

91
00:07:35,010 --> 00:07:40,250
Most liquids leave a film on the inside wall of a new tip, which affects the actual volume

92
00:07:40,250 --> 00:07:42,170
that can be transferred.

93
00:07:42,170 --> 00:07:46,630
By pre-rinsing a tip, these volume variances are eliminated.

94
00:07:46,630 --> 00:07:52,950
To pre-rinse a tip, simply draw a solution into the tip and expel it into a waste container.

95
00:07:52,950 --> 00:07:56,330
Third, check the immersion depth.

96
00:07:56,330 --> 00:08:00,910
If the immersion depth is too deep, liquid may get into the plastic shaft and damage

97
00:08:00,910 --> 00:08:03,470
the mechanics of the pipette men.

98
00:08:03,470 --> 00:08:07,950
If the immersion depth is too shallow, air may be drawn into the tip, which greatly

99
00:08:07,950 --> 00:08:10,030
affects accuracy.

100
00:08:10,030 --> 00:08:15,750
As a general rule, immerse the white tips to a depth of 1-2 milliliters, the yellow

101
00:08:15,750 --> 00:08:22,830
tips to a depth of 2-3 milliliters, and the blue tips to a depth of 2-4 millimeters.

102
00:08:22,830 --> 00:08:28,550
If you do find an air bubble in the tip, dispense the sample into a waste container.

103
00:08:28,550 --> 00:08:32,270
Check your immersion level and pipette more slowly.

104
00:08:32,270 --> 00:08:37,390
If you still see an air bubble, discard your current tip and use a new one.

105
00:08:37,390 --> 00:08:41,550
Fourth, release the plunger button slowly.

106
00:08:41,550 --> 00:08:43,950
Do not let the plunger snap up.

107
00:08:43,950 --> 00:08:47,790
This violent action may force liquid into the plastic shaft.

108
00:08:47,790 --> 00:08:54,150
Fifth, when wiping excess liquid off the sides of the tip, avoid touching the tip opening

109
00:08:54,150 --> 00:08:56,910
because some of the sample may be lost.

110
00:08:56,910 --> 00:09:03,070
Sixth, when liquid is in its tip, never allow the pipette men to tilt more than 20 degrees

111
00:09:03,070 --> 00:09:08,870
from vertical and never lay the pipette men on its side.

112
00:09:08,870 --> 00:09:13,750
For optimum reproducibility, it is important to be consistent with your samples.

113
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:19,310
Specifically, use a consistent speed and smoothness when pressing and releasing the push button.

114
00:09:19,310 --> 00:09:24,430
Use consistent pressure at the first step, immerse the tip to a consistent depth, and

115
00:09:24,430 --> 00:09:31,260
hold the pipette men at a consistent angle that is less than 20 degrees from vertical.

116
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:37,180
If you are confident that you used good technique and are still seeing errors with accuracy or

117
00:09:37,180 --> 00:09:43,540
precision, the following troubleshooting guidelines may fix your problem.

118
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:47,940
To ensure that your pipette men is actually delivering the desired volume, you should

119
00:09:47,940 --> 00:09:50,540
periodically calibrate it.

120
00:09:50,540 --> 00:09:56,060
To calibrate a pipette men, determine how many grams of your liquid equals one milliliter

121
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:57,940
from its density.

122
00:09:57,940 --> 00:10:03,540
For example, if water is the solvent, then one milliliter of water equals one gram of

123
00:10:03,540 --> 00:10:04,860
water.

124
00:10:04,860 --> 00:10:10,060
Tear a small container on an analytical balance and set the pipette men at a volume near the

125
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:12,380
top of its range.

126
00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:16,660
Transfer the set volume of liquid to the tared container on the balance and note the

127
00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:19,260
mass of the liquid.

128
00:10:19,260 --> 00:10:24,220
Repeat this step five more times and determine the mean weight and standard deviation from

129
00:10:24,220 --> 00:10:26,220
your measurements.

130
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:30,620
Repeat the entire process with the pipette men set at a volume near the bottom of its

131
00:10:30,620 --> 00:10:32,140
range.

132
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:37,500
If these values deviate from what you expect, adjust the pipette men accordingly when you

133
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:39,420
transfer liquid.

134
00:10:39,420 --> 00:10:44,860
Occasionally, the pipette men will begin to leak due to improperly fitted tips or worn

135
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:46,180
o-rings.

136
00:10:46,180 --> 00:10:51,900
To check for leaks, put a fresh tip on the pipette men, pre-rinse the tip, and draw

137
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:53,660
up liquid.

138
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:57,700
Keep the pipette men vertical and wait about 20 seconds.

139
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:02,500
If you see a droplet, then the pipette men is leaking.

140
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:07,140
Two final things that decrease the accuracy and precision of the pipette men involve

141
00:11:07,140 --> 00:11:12,500
pipetting samples below 4 or above 70 degrees Celsius and using the pipette men with

142
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:14,460
acids and corrosives.

143
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:18,940
If you absolutely must pipette an acid or corrosive, then you should disassemble the

144
00:11:18,940 --> 00:11:24,260
pipette men immediately to inspect and clean the piston, shaft, seal assemblies, and

145
00:11:24,260 --> 00:11:26,260
o-rings.

146
00:11:26,260 --> 00:11:31,860
To summarize, using proper technique with an automatic pipette is essential for ensuring

147
00:11:31,860 --> 00:11:34,300
accuracy and precision.

148
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:37,740
A few key points to remember include the following.

149
00:11:37,740 --> 00:11:43,660
Be consistent with plunger timing and pressure, as well as immersion time and depth.

150
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:47,660
Use a fresh pre-rinse tip for each new sample.

151
00:11:47,660 --> 00:11:53,060
Check for air bubbles and leaks during each transfer and calibrate the automatic pipette

152
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:54,820
periodically.

153
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:59,740
In addition, take care not to drop the expense of an easily damaged pipette men.

154
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:03,000
Do not allow the tip to touch anything except sample.

155
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,980
Do not introduce liquid into the plastic shaft by letting the plunger snap up or

156
00:12:06,980 --> 00:12:09,580
by laying the pipette men on its side.

157
00:12:09,580 --> 00:12:14,020
Finally, avoid using the pipette men under extreme temperature conditions or with acids

158
00:12:14,020 --> 00:12:17,360
and corrosives.

159
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:22,720
When used with consistency and care, an automatic pipette is an excellent means of transferring

160
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:24,600
microliter quantities of liquid.
